! AlAs, **PAW**, zincblende, at `alas-piezo-tiny.in`'s cutoff and mesh: the cell
! that puts the one dataset kind whose paths were live and untested under a test.
!
! The wedge completion the piezoelectric tape gained on 2026-09-19 runs for PAW
! as well as for ultrasoft -- `_full_zone_becsum_response` is the PAW half of it
! -- and no non-centrosymmetric PAW crystal was committed, so the branch that
! carries `becsum` through the completion had never executed. The datasets here
! are the PAW counterparts of the ultrasoft pair beside it, so the two cells
! differ in the dataset kind and in nothing else at all: same geometry, same
! `celldm`, same cutoffs, same unshifted `2 2 2` grid.
!
! Nothing about the *value* of `e_14` here is physics, for the reason
! `alas-piezo-tiny.in` gives at length: `ecutwfc = 10` with `ecutrho = 44` is far
! below what `Al/As.pbe-n-kjpaw_psl.1.0.0` were made for. What the cell keeps is
! what the identity needs -- an augmented dataset with one-centre terms, on a
! crystal whose tensor does not vanish by symmetry. As a check that the cheap
! cutoff has not made it a different problem, the dielectric constant reads
! 42.05 here against the ultrasoft cell's 42.16.
!
! `nosym` and `noinv` on an unshifted grid, so this is the **closed grid**: eight
! k-points and no symmetrisation, which is what `alas-piezo-tiny-paw-wedge.in` is
! compared against.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.6134, nat = 2, ntyp = 2,
    ecutwfc = 10.0, ecutrho = 44.0
    nosym = .true.
    noinv = .true.
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Al 26.98 Al.pbe-n-kjpaw_psl.1.0.0.UPF
 As 74.92 As.pbe-n-kjpaw_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
 Al 0.00 0.00 0.00
 As 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 2 2 2 0 0 0
